<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Malla republic</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.) · capital Kushinagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A clan republic of the Ganges plain in whose town of Kushinagar the Buddha died.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mallas were a warrior clan whose land lay east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kosala</a>, in modern eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. By the 6th century BCE they formed a gana-sangha, a republic of leading families, and were counted among the sixteen mahajanapadas. They were divided into two branches, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a> and Pava.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Affairs were decided by assemblies of clan heads meeting in a hall called the santhagara. Buddhist texts describe how the Mallas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a> received the dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>, cremated him and shared his relics, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> tradition places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira's</a> death at Pava. Mallas were famed as wrestlers and soldiers, and Bandhula, a Malla, served as general to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasenajit">Prasenajit</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kingdom of Kosala</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by internal division and pressed by stronger neighbours, the Mallas were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a> in the 5th or 4th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a> became one of the four main places of Buddhist pilgrimage.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 700 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kushinagar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Jainism, Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malla Mahajanapada, Mallas of Kushinagar</dd></dl></aside></div>

